/* Maui personality rebuild chapters 31–40: stay bold as trust matures. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const edits={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-31":{"number":31,"title":"Six Different Roads","content":"By the time I returned to Tideglass, I had tea leaves in one pocket, a spare bolt in another, and three appointments written on the back of a bakery receipt.\n\nMaui examined the collection when I emptied it onto the kitchen table.\n\n\"Did you rob the village?\"\n\n\"Only the parts that were freely offered.\"\n\nHe held up the bolt.\n\n\"Romantic.\"\n\n\"Danse thought it might help Tamsin with the shore gate.\"\n\n\"The tea?\"\n\n\"Iroh gave me the last of the house blend for Fen.\"\n\n\"And the receipt?\"\n\n\"That's my calendar, apparently.\"\n\nHe sat down.\n\n\"You're going to need a larger pocket.\"\n\nI laughed, then stopped. I was exhausted in a way sleep alone wouldn't fix. I'd spent the last few days moving between districts, helping with the storm repairs and trying to keep promises to people I loved.\n\nI wanted to tell Maui everything.\n\nI didn't know where to begin.\n\nHe slid a cup toward me.\n\n\"Sit.\"\n\nI sat.\n\n\"Tell me the part I should know,\" he said.\n\nNot the part he had a right to own. Not every conversation, kiss, disagreement, or private fear from the other relationships.\n\nThe part I wanted to share.\n\nI wrapped my hands around the cup.\n\n\"Severus and Neri are still reviewing the Closed Bells records. They found a scraped signature, but they won't guess whose it was.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Iroh's terrace drains were making strange sounds before the storm. He thinks the blocked service route may be pushing water into places it never used to go.\"\n\n\"Less good.\"\n\n\"Danse and Toph disagree about the shore gate, but they're finally using the same measurements.\"\n\nMaui smiled.\n\n\"That sounds like a miracle.\"\n\n\"Don't call it that. They'll both be offended.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI told him Katara had returned from the South and was helping the clinic organize temporary supplies across the closed bridge. Aang was making sure the emergency travel rules didn't become an excuse to control people. Greg had offered to bring chairs and lanterns to the hall, but only after checking with the volunteers.\n\n\"And you?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"What about me?\"\n\n\"What did you need?\"\n\nI looked at the table.\n\nThat was the question I'd been avoiding.\n\nI had spent so much energy helping other people have boundaries that I had begun treating my own as a nuisance.\n\n\"I need a day,\" I admitted. \"A day where nobody asks me to hold a key, fix a schedule, or make a decision.\"\n\nMaui nodded.\n\n\"Then let's have one.\"\n\n\"I don't want to cancel plans.\"\n\n\"Do you need to?\"\n\nI unfolded the bakery receipt. Two commitments could be moved without anyone suffering; the third was a clinic delivery that somebody else had already offered to cover.\n\nI wrote three short notes.\n\nThen I looked up.\n\n\"Are you sure you want to spend a day with me doing nothing useful?\"\n\nMaui grinned.\n\n\"I am a demigod. I can survive boredom.\"\n\n\"That remains untested.\"\n\nWe sent the notes before I could change my mind.\n\nThe next morning we slept late.\n\nThere were no bells. No urgent knocks. The sea was quiet enough that I could hear the wind moving through the little gaps around the window frame. Maui made breakfast with too much cinnamon and blamed the spoon.\n\nI ate it anyway.\n\nWe walked along the upper ridge, where the storm had left seaweed tangled around the stones. The lower gate remained closed for inspection. The new garden beds were damp but intact, and Fen's little patch of wild flowers had survived.\n\nMaui stopped beside the bench.\n\n\"Can I ask something difficult?\" he said.\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Do you ever wish there were only one road?\"\n\nI looked out over the cove.\n\n\"Sometimes I wish the roads weren't so complicated.\"\n\n\"That's not what I asked.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe waited.\n\nI spread the receipt on the table. The back was covered in small, unromantic obligations: a confirmed visit with Iroh, a message to Severus about the archive, a possible afternoon at Brasswatch, and a reminder to ask Katara whether the clinic still needed the clean blankets.\n\nMaui stared. \"It looks like an invasion plan.\"\n\nHe spread the receipt across the table as though it were a map of enemy ships.\n\n\"Look at this. Six roads, three people who apparently have excellent taste in partners, two meetings, and one dinner with the most impressive demigod in existence.\"\n\n\"Is that last one in the schedule?\"\n\n\"I just added it. In very large letters.\"\n\nMini Maui climbed across his chest, inspected the receipt, and made a tiny face of outrage at the amount of time left blank.\n\n\"Don't start,\" Maui told him. \"Blank time is not an insult.\"\n\n\"Do you believe that?\" I asked.\n\nHe flashed me a dazzling grin. \"Absolutely.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"Mostly. I am working up to absolutely.\"\n\nHe had once pulled islands from the sea. I knew it from his stories, and from the ridiculous pride with which he told them. But a calendar full of people I loved frightened him more than any creature he'd fought. He wanted to feel chosen without asking me to make anybody else disappear. The fact he could laugh at his own fear didn't make it less real.\n\n\"It's a calendar.\"\n\n\"Those are nearly the same.\"\n\nI laughed and smoothed the curling paper. He leaned close without trying to read the parts I kept folded.\n\n\"Which of these actually affects us?\" he asked.\n\n\"The visit I promised you on Saturday. The dinner I said I'd cook tonight. And the fact that I won't be here tomorrow morning.\"\n\nHe nodded, trying to take in the difference between knowing the important plans and knowing everything. We'd talked openly about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory; the trick was living that agreement when feelings weren't tidy.\n\n\"Can I say I want more of your Saturdays?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. And I can tell you which ones I want to spend here.\"\n\n\"What if I don't like the answer?\"\n\n\"Then we can be disappointed without punishing each other.\"\n\nHe considered that for a while. \"Do you ever feel guilty because there are six roads?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. Usually when I imagine somebody wants me to solve their sadness by giving away mine.\"\n\nMaui's expression softened. \"That sounds exhausting.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe fetched the bowl of sea-glass stones we'd collected along the shore. He picked one, worn smooth and shaped a little like a heart, and set it beside the receipt.\n\n\"I don't want to be a road you have to walk because you promised.\"\n\n\"Then don't make yourself one.\"\n\n\"Could I be a place you choose to rest?\"\n\nThe question caught me off guard. I reached for his hand.\n\n\"You already are,\" I said.\n\nHe smiled, quieter than usual. For a moment the kitchen was only a kitchen, full of crumbs and papers and two people learning not to count affection like a debt. Then he asked whose turn it was to wash the dishes, and the moment improved considerably.\n\nI thought about the people I loved. Iroh's patient warmth. Severus's precise questions. Katara's steadiness, Aang's hope, and Toph's refusal to let anyone flatter her into silence. Danse learning to be a protector without a rank. Greg finding music in the quiet. Maui standing beside me, trying not to turn his own fear into a demand.\n\nNone of them was interchangeable.\n\n\"Do you wish I loved only you?\" I asked.\n\nHe looked down.\n\n\"Sometimes I wish loving you were easier.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\n\"But that's different from wanting you to be somebody else.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe stared at our fingers.\n\n\"I don't need to know everything you do with everyone else,\" he said. \"I want to know what affects us. When plans change. When you're afraid. When you want me close or need me to step back.\"\n\n\"That sounds fair.\"\n\n\"And I want you to tell me when I'm asking for too much.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n\"Even if I complain?\"\n\n\"Especially then.\"\n\nWe sat on the bench for a long time.\n\nThe sea looked deceptively smooth from the ridge. Out beyond the harbor, the current still moved toward the old overflow channel. The second bell had not sounded since the storm.\n\nI wasn't going to pretend that meant it was safe.\n\nMaui leaned against me.\n\n\"Tell me something that has nothing to do with the village,\" he said.\n\nI thought about it.\n\n\"Greg wrote a song that starts with the sound of a ferry bell.\"\n\n\"That's still the village.\"\n\n\"Severus puts his books in order by a system that makes sense only to him.\"\n\n\"That's probably a crime.\"\n\n\"Toph called my kite a potato.\"\n\nHe laughed so suddenly I nearly spilled my tea.\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"A graceful potato.\"\n\n\"Now I need to see it.\"\n\nI pulled the little sketch from my notebook. He studied it with great seriousness.\n\n\"Terrible,\" he said.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Absolutely terrible.\"\n\nI kissed him.\n\nHe caught my hand and kissed me again, slow and warm. The breeze lifted my hair, and the sun moved across the cove. For once I didn't feel the need to explain how this moment fit into the rest of my life.\n\nIt was part of it.\n\nOn the way home we stopped at the market. Mara had fresh bread. Fen was arguing with a supplier about the cost of rope. Lio showed us the corrected seat on the skiff and asked Maui to check a knot.\n\nHe did, then handed the line back.\n\nNo grand rescue.\n\nAt home, I took the bakery receipt from my pocket and copied the appointments into the proper calendar. I left a blank day for myself next week, without filling it immediately.\n\nMaui noticed.\n\n\"Another day of doing nothing?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThat night I wrote a note in the green notebook I'd found in my old room.\n\nI am not divided into six people because I love more than one.\n\nI am one person with different roads to walk, and I get to decide when to rest.\n\nMaui read the note only after I invited him.\n\nHe didn't add a speech.\n\nHe drew a tiny fish underneath, carrying a very large cup of tea.\n\nI laughed and closed the book.","word_count":1698},"maui-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Shape of an Agreement","content":"The calendar had survived salt spray, one dropped kettle, and three months of Maui writing appointments in letters large enough to be seen from the harbor. It did not survive his attempt to improve it.\n\nI found it on the kitchen table with a line drawn through Thursday.\n\nThursday had been ours.\n\nHe had replaced it with a note: *Helping Fen. Necessary.*\n\nI stood there holding a parcel from Moonroot Archive, still wearing the coat I'd put on that morning to come home early. We'd planned the evening two weeks ago. Not a festival, not a rescue, not a shift at the pantry. Dinner, the little blue blanket, and absolutely no public meetings.\n\n\"Maui?\"\n\nHe came in carrying a coil of rope. \"Before you say anything, the new mooring—\"\n\n\"You canceled our evening.\"\n\nHe looked at the calendar. \"Moved it. Friday's open.\"\n\n\"Friday Iroh and I are reviewing the terrace map.\"\n\n\"Then Saturday.\"\n\n\"Saturday you promised Lio you'd inspect the skiff.\"\n\nHis smile faltered. \"Right.\"\n\nI set the parcel beside the stove. The air smelled like wet rope and rosemary. I wanted to say something spectacularly unfair, something about being last on his list. A familiar, uglier part of me wanted to win the argument before he knew it had begun.\n\nInstead I said, \"You didn't ask me.\"\n\nMaui put down the rope. \"Fen needed someone to help secure the landing before the wind shifts.\"\n\n\"Fen could need help. I might even agree that it's urgent. Neither changes the fact that you decided what happened to my evening without telling me.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd understand.\"\n\n\"I do understand the mooring. I don't understand why you didn't send a note.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. \"I was busy.\"\n\n\"So busy you had time to rewrite the calendar.\"\n\nThat landed.\n\nHe looked at the enormous letters he'd written across Thursday. \"Okay. That was bad.\"\n\nI pulled out a chair. \"Sit with me?\"\n\nHe sat, awkwardly. His injured hand had healed, but he still curled his fingers when he was ashamed, as if bracing for a blow that wasn't coming.\n\n\"I made a mistake,\" he said. \"I should have asked.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"I need to know something, too.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"Sometimes I look at that calendar and feel like I'm asking for an appointment with someone who has six lives. Then I hate myself for thinking it, because you told me from the beginning that you're ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I agreed to that knowing what it meant.\"\n\n\"I don't have six lives,\" I said. \"I have one. It's the same me coming to your door.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Knowing doesn't mean you stop feeling lonely.\"\n\nHe looked at me, surprised.\n\nI turned the calendar around. Its edges were curled. Under my appointments he'd added tiny drawings of fish and hooks. Beneath my private time with Severus, he'd written *No questions*. He had been trying, in his own untidy way, to respect agreements he didn't always find easy.\n\n\"Can we write down what we actually mean?\" I asked.\n\n\"Without making it a contract that gets me arrested?\"\n\n\"Preferably.\"\n\nWe took a clean sheet from the green notebook. Across the top I wrote *Our present agreements—not ownership, not forever.*\n\nMaui leaned over to read it.\n\n\"That's a long title.\"\n\n\"We can edit.\"\n\nWe agreed to tell each other about schedule changes before altering shared plans, unless there was an immediate emergency. In that case we would send word as soon as safely possible. We agreed not to share details about my other partners' private lives without their permission. He could ask for reassurance, and I could answer honestly without ranking anybody.\n\nMost importantly, either of us could say no to a date, a conversation, a kiss, or a request for time. No explanation was required to make a no count.\n\nMaui studied the last line.\n\n\"If you say no because you need quiet, can I ask when we might talk again?\"\n\n\"Yes. That's a request, not a demand. Sometimes I won't know.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"And if I need quiet?\"\n\n\"Then you get it too.\"\n\nWe tried the new agreement on a small problem before declaring it useful.\n\n\"Suppose Fen needs you during our next dinner,\" I said.\n\nMaui looked at the list. \"I ask whether it's an emergency.\"\n\n\"And if it is?\"\n\n\"I tell you where I'm going if there's time. I send word when I can. I don't decide what you'll do with the evening.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"And if it isn't?\"\n\n\"You can still choose to help, but you ask whether we can move our date before you strike it through.\"\n\nHe made a face. \"That means you might say no.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Terrible system.\"\n\nI poked him with the pencil. \"Try again.\"\n\nHe laughed. \"That means I need to decide what promise I'm keeping instead of assuming you'll wait.\"\n\nThe distinction mattered more than I expected. He could care about Fen, and he could care about me. Nobody had to become the villain because two good things asked for the same hour.\n\nI had told him I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory, but a relationship agreement wasn't just about who we kissed. It was about whether he could need reassurance without demanding my freedom, and whether I could choose to be with him without pretending all my other commitments had vanished.\n\n\"Would you like me to come with you to check the mooring tomorrow?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes. If you want to.\"\n\n\"I can do the morning. In the afternoon I'm at Moonroot.\"\n\nHe wrote both parts down. His handwriting was so large that the last word disappeared into the margin.\n\n\"There's no room,\" he complained.\n\n\"Then we'll need another page.\"\n\nHe looked at the green notebook and reached for a blank sheet. \"Are you sure we can change this later?\"\n\n\"We have to be able to. Otherwise it becomes another door we're afraid to open.\"\n\nHe nodded, and when I leaned close he waited for me to kiss him. The warmth of his hand at my waist felt like an invitation I was still free to accept tomorrow or refuse next week. That made it sweeter, not smaller.\n\nWe left space at the bottom to revise the page. We signed it with our names, not as a legal instrument but because the act made us both laugh. Maui drew a small fish beside his signature. Its expression was deeply suspicious.\n\nThe fish beside his name looked as though it were preparing to attack the calendar.\n\n\"That's you,\" I said.\n\nMaui drew himself up. \"That is a fearsome guardian of the Thursday dinner.\"\n\n\"It's a fish with a grudge.\"\n\n\"Exactly. Deadly.\"\n\nMini Maui poked his head out of the tattoos and began miming the fish's angry expression. The real Maui looked down and pointed a warning finger.\n\n\"No sabotage. We are handling this like adults.\"\n\n\"You sound disappointed.\"\n\n\"I am extremely disappointed. Handling things like adults is exhausting. Do you know how much easier it is to fight a monster?\"\n\nI laughed. He grinned with genuine relief, then tapped the crossed-out Thursday and the newly marked Sunday.\n\n\"Still want that dinner with me?\" he asked, quieter.\n\n\"Yes. On Sunday.\"\n\n\"Good. I'm going to make something so magnificent you'll write songs about it.\"\n\n\"I'll settle for edible.\"\n\nHis offended gasp echoed into the kitchen. He left for Fen's shift on time, but not before drawing a tiny crown on the fish.\n\nI glanced at the clock.\n\n\"You still have the mooring shift?\"\n\n\"Yes. Fen's expecting me.\"\n\n\"Then go. I don't want the landing to fail because we were arguing.\"\n\nHe paused at the door. \"Are we all right?\"\n\n\"We're working on it.\"\n\nHe seemed to take that more seriously than any grand assurance I could have given.\n\nThe next morning he sent a message before the agreed hour. Fen's inspection had uncovered a worn ring. They would need another half-day, and he wanted to know whether I minded moving our meal to Sunday.\n\nI smiled at the message.\n\n*Sunday works. Ask Lio whether Saturday still suits them.*\n\nHe answered with a picture of a fish whose fins were holding a calendar.\n\nOn Sunday, we made dinner together. Maui burned the first flatbread. I misread the spice jar and filled the kitchen with cinnamon. We ate the second batch outside by the sheltered garden, where the sea kale was growing slowly but stubbornly.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" he asked when we'd cleared the plates.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHis thumb brushed my cheek. He kissed me as though he didn't want the moment to finish, then drew back to ask whether I was cold.\n\n\"A little,\" I admitted.\n\nHe fetched the blue blanket.\n\nWe sat close, the sea breathing beyond the stones, and I let my head rest against him. Later we closed our door and kept the evening to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning the calendar was still hanging. Thursday was crossed out, Sunday carried a small blue star, and our new agreement lay beside the green notebook, ready to be revised when life made more honesty necessary.\n\nIt was paper.\n\nWe were the people who had to keep choosing what it meant.","word_count":1523},"maui-33":{"number":33,"title":"The Unfinished Song","content":"Greg had written four lines and crossed out three.\n\nHe showed me the page without pretending it was finished.\n\n\"Something's missing,\" he said.\n\nThe Starwake Pier stage was empty that morning. The western boards had reopened after inspection, but the public performance area wasn't booked until the weekend. A breeze rattled the spare chairs in the storage shed. Greg had brought his repaired guitar; the scratch along the body still caught the light.\n\nMaui followed us down the pier carrying a basket of breakfast rolls.\n\n\"Great!\" he said when Greg explained the problem. \"I'll give you a verse about the sea. Storms, great deeds, an enormous hero—\"\n\n\"I was hoping for something smaller,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Small hero?\"\n\n\"No hero, actually.\"\n\nMaui looked personally wounded.\n\nMini Maui appeared on his chest, threw up both arms as if Greg had insulted the entire history of heroes, and pointed toward the sea for dramatic effect.\n\nMaui gave the tattoo an appreciative nod. \"Exactly! You ask the demigod with the greatest maritime résumé ever assembled for a song and then say you don't need the hero part?\"\n\nGreg plucked one note. \"I asked you to hang out, man.\"\n\n\"Which I am also excellent at. Some would say legendary.\"\n\n\"You can be legendary at not interrupting for a minute.\"\n\nMaui put a finger to his lips. The tattoo mimed him with such exaggerated innocence that I knew silence wouldn't last long. I caught Greg smiling into the guitar. There was something affectionate in how he let Maui be ridiculous without rewarding the bid to make every conversation about his deeds.\n\nA gull cried above the rail. Maui looked up, briefly considering a bird transformation.\n\n\"Don't,\" I whispered.\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"You absolutely were.\"\n\nHe flashed a grin. Greg found the next note without either of us needing to explain why the silence had mattered.\n\nGreg played the four-note phrase again, the one he'd built from the ferry bell at Tideglass. The fifth note rose and settled in a way that reminded me of somebody returning after a long absence.\n\n\"See?\" he said. \"After that it needs a breath, but whenever I write words, I fill it up.\"\n\nMaui leaned on the railing. \"The problem is you're thinking too quietly. The ocean—\"\n\n\"Can you just sit with me for a while?\" Greg interrupted.\n\nThere was nothing angry in his voice. He looked tired.\n\nMaui stared. \"You don't need me to do anything?\"\n\n\"I asked because I like having you around. You're good at looking at the water without getting bored.\"\n\n\"I'm also good at a lot of other things.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThe silence that followed made me want to jump in and explain both men to each other. Maui was trying to contribute; Greg wanted company. They could probably solve it themselves.\n\nSo I carried the breakfast basket to the bench and passed out the rolls.\n\nGreg played again.\n\nMaui lasted almost two minutes without speaking. Then he pointed toward the far end of the pier.\n\n\"That board is loose.\"\n\n\"It passed inspection,\" I said.\n\n\"It's making a sound.\"\n\nGreg stopped playing. \"What kind of sound?\"\n\nMaui tapped the railing once, listening. \"Like the line on a boat when it starts taking weight. Not danger exactly. Just something asking to be checked.\"\n\nGreg looked at his guitar. \"Huh.\"\n\nI knew the look. He'd found a thought he wanted to keep.\n\n\"Don't make him sing about the board,\" I warned.\n\nGreg grinned. \"I might.\"\n\nMaui looked pleased. \"Finally. A subject worthy of a song.\"\n\nThe three of us sat until the wind shifted. We listened to ropes clicking against cleats and water moving below the supports. The low note from the old drainage passage did not sound; Fen had recorded an ordinary tide that morning. There was no mystery demanding we stop enjoying breakfast.\n\nAfter a while Greg asked Maui, \"What's the sound you miss most from home?\"\n\nMaui didn't answer immediately.\n\nThen he said, \"The moment just before a wave breaks on the reef. People hear the crash. I hear the water pulling itself together.\"\n\nGreg nodded and played a note without words.\n\nMaui listened.\n\n\"That one,\" he said quietly.\n\nGreg tried it again. He moved two fingers along the strings, leaving a longer gap after the fifth note.\n\nI could see Maui wanting to fill it. He opened his mouth, closed it, and looked down at his hands.\n\n\"Do you want me to do something?\" he finally asked.\n\n\"Stay,\" Greg said.\n\nMaui nodded.\n\nThe answer was almost painfully simple.\n\nBefore Steven came, Tamsin walked down the safe side of the pier with a clipboard tucked beneath her arm. She heard the same faint rattle Maui had pointed out and stopped.\n\n\"Good ear,\" she told him. \"It passed the earlier check, but this wind is different. I'll log it before the next set.\"\n\nMaui looked so pleased that Greg grinned.\n\n\"See? You helped without writing me a sea-king anthem.\"\n\n\"I still have excellent rhymes available.\"\n\n\"Keep them for later.\"\n\nTamsin tested the cleat and marked the board for a second inspection before the weekend performance. It wasn't an emergency, and she didn't need the whole stage closed. The tiny distinction mattered: noticing a problem wasn't the same as declaring everything ruined, and somebody qualified had to decide what the sound meant.\n\nGreg stopped playing until Tamsin finished. He asked whether the inspection would change his rehearsal time and offered to move to the sheltered east bench if it did. She said that would be sensible, thanked him, and went back to her work.\n\nNola came up the pier with a coil of yellow rope over one shoulder. The knot in it was so complicated I couldn't tell whether it had been tied on purpose.\n\n\"You're moving?\" she asked Greg.\n\n\"Temporarily. Tamsin's got a second look at the cleat.\"\n\n\"Good. I need the east bench for the afternoon instrument return. If you can finish before then, you're welcome to use it.\"\n\nGreg checked his watch. \"Thirty minutes?\"\n\n\"Twenty-five.\"\n\nHe made a face. \"You're a tough booking agent.\"\n\n\"I'm the one who puts the chairs away when the booking agent goes home.\"\n\nMaui reached for her rope. \"I can carry that.\"\n\n\"You can ask where it goes,\" Nola said, passing it to him.\n\nI hid my smile behind a breakfast roll. He'd been about to offer to carry every rope on the pier as though strength were the only thing the place lacked.\n\nGreg sat on the steps with his notebook balanced on his knee. The unfinished song had room for another line, but his pencil hovered over the page.\n\n\"What if the missing part isn't words?\" Maui asked.\n\nGreg looked up. \"Go on.\"\n\nMaui glanced at the loose cleat, the empty stage, and Nola dragging a chair into storage. \"You stop playing when somebody needs to say something. Maybe the song needs somewhere to stop.\"\n\nGreg stared at him.\n\n\"That is either beautiful or incredibly annoying,\" he said.\n\n\"Both,\" I offered.\n\nHe laughed, then struck a chord and let it fade without filling the silence. The ropes creaked softly, and somewhere underneath the boards a gull complained about the morning.\n\n\"Yeah,\" Greg said. \"That's closer.\"\n\nMaui was grinning so hard it almost hurt to look at him. He had helped make music without demanding that anybody sing about him. Nola called from the shed to remind us that twenty-five minutes did not expand just because the tune was improving.\n\nGreg closed his notebook and picked up the guitar. He had a deadline. The unfinished line could wait for him.\n\nWe carried the guitar case to the bench. Maui looked at the cleared stage.\n\n\"I thought the sound was only something to fix.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it's a piece of a song,\" Greg said. \"Sometimes it's a safety problem. You can listen for both.\"\n\nMaui considered that, then leaned against the railing with his breakfast roll.\n\nI wanted to say something important about the way he was learning. Instead I asked if he wanted the last piece of bread.\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nGreg held out his hand for it first. Maui stared at him in betrayal. They argued cheerfully over the roll until I tore it in half, which struck me as considerably better music than a speech about sharing.\n\nAround midday Steven arrived carrying a small tin of snacks. He waved to me, hugged Greg, and asked whether he was interrupting.\n\n\"Not at all,\" Greg said. \"We're doing complicated artistic work.\"\n\nSteven looked at the three of us sitting silently.\n\n\"Looks intense.\"\n\n\"Very,\" Maui agreed.\n\nSteven laughed and settled at the edge of the bench. He didn't ask to hear a private song before it was ready. Greg played a bit of the new melody because he wanted to, not because his son had to be an audience.\n\nBy late afternoon the song still had only two lines.\n\nGreg looked happier.\n\nOn the walk back to Tideglass, Maui carried the empty basket. He had a strange expression, as though he'd done a difficult task and wasn't sure whether anyone had noticed.\n\n\"Did you enjoy today?\" I asked.\n\n\"I was waiting for Greg to need me.\"\n\n\"He did.\"\n\n\"I didn't fix anything.\"\n\n\"You listened.\"\n\n\"Very heroic.\"\n\n\"Not everything is supposed to be.\"\n\nHe stopped beside the sea-glass garden. The raised beds were full of the tiny, surviving signs of the season. A white flower bent in the salt wind but did not break.\n\n\"Sometimes I worry,\" he said, \"that if I'm not doing something remarkable, you won't need me.\"\n\nI took his hand. \"I don't want you because you're necessary. I want you because I like the person who sits beside me, even when there's nothing to rescue.\"\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\n\"That person says very few intelligent things.\"\n\n\"True.\"\n\n\"Terrible cook.\"\n\n\"Frequently.\"\n\n\"Excellent at carrying blankets.\"\n\n\"Legendary.\"\n\nHe laughed and drew me close enough to kiss. The kiss was gentle, a little shy, warmed by the long ordinary day.\n\nLater Greg sent a note. He had kept the space between the notes, written one new line, and left the song unfinished.\n\nBeneath it he added: *Tell Maui thanks for sitting with me.*\n\nI showed Maui.\n\nHe read it twice.\n\nThen he put the note beside the tiny wooden fish, where he kept things that made him remember the difference between being admired and being wanted.\n\nThat night he didn't tell me a heroic story.\n\nWe listened to the sea until we were ready to go inside.","word_count":1745},"maui-34":{"number":34,"title":"A Nail from the Commonwealth","content":"The new fastener looked perfect until I pulled it from the board.\n\nOrange rust coated the threads. The metal was rough where it should have been smooth, and the hole in the timber had widened enough for seawater to gather around it.\n\nMaui stared at it.\n\n\"That came from Danse.\"\n\n\"From Brasswatch,\" I corrected. \"It might have come through his workshop. That doesn't make it his fault.\"\n\n\"It was supposed to last.\"\n\n\"So was the old rope.\"\n\nFen examined the piece under a magnifying lens. We'd found it during the morning inspection at the lower landing, where Lio's skiff had begun knocking against a repair bracket. Nobody had been injured. We had taken the mooring out of service immediately.\n\nTamsin joined us with the purchase record.\n\n\"Salvage fastener,\" she read. \"Recovered from a dry storage shed in the Commonwealth. Inspected for shape and load.\"\n\n\"Not for salt,\" Fen said.\n\nTamsin closed her eyes.\n\n\"Not for salt.\"\n\nMaui folded his arms. \"Why didn't anyone check?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe winced. \"Right. That's the question. Not the accusation.\"\n\nWe carried the rusted nail to Brasswatch.\n\nDanse was teaching Renn how to test a water pump. He took one look at the fastener and put the training piece aside.\n\n\"That's not acceptable,\" he said.\n\n\"Can you tell us why?\" I asked.\n\nHe turned the nail under the light.\n\n\"Corrosion. The protective coating was already compromised. Dry use might not have exposed it quickly, but coastal spray would.\"\n\nToph arrived from the next workroom, following the sound of our conversation.\n\n\"Something's wrong with the dock?\"\n\n\"One bracket,\" Tamsin said. \"The rest is being inspected.\"\n\nToph held the fastener carefully, testing its weight. \"This isn't magic, is it?\"\n\nDanse looked offended. \"No.\"\n\n\"Good. I hate when people make bad engineering interesting by calling it a curse.\"\n\nRenn fetched the batch ledger. There were twelve nails from the same salvaged lot. Five had been used at Tideglass, three on a protected shed in Brasswatch, and four remained in storage.\n\n\"We need all of them identified,\" Danse said.\n\nMaui looked toward the door. \"I'll go tear them out.\"\n\nHis hook was already in his hand. For one horrible instant I could picture him ripping half the pier to pieces in pursuit of a problem that was holding the other half up.\n\n\"Great Maui to the rescue,\" he announced through clenched teeth. \"One hundred rusty nails, zero survivors.\"\n\n\"Five installed here,\" Danse corrected, looking at the ledger. \"Twelve in the batch.\"\n\n\"You're ruining the rhythm.\"\n\nMini Maui appeared beside the hook tattoo and drew a small diagram of a collapsing dock. The larger Maui stared at it, then at the real water below the window.\n\n\"Okay, yes,\" he muttered. \"Maybe not the whole dock.\"\n\nHe had extraordinary strength and absolutely no patience for watching a problem become a spreadsheet. The waiting made him twitch. Yet when Tamsin asked him to brace a beam while she checked the replacement, he planted himself there and held it as steadily as a mountain.\n\n\"Just so we're clear,\" he told me, \"I am still the strongest person here.\"\n\n\"Nobody disputed that.\"\n\n\"Good. Because I needed to hear it.\"\n\nHe grinned. The grin was not an apology for being wrong. The decision to wait was.\n\n\"Not all at once,\" Tamsin said. \"Some are holding things in place until we brace the structure.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nWe made a list of locations and assigned inspection teams. Fen closed the affected landing section. Tamsin designed temporary supports. Danse checked the material specification and compared replacement options. Renn contacted the supplier without accusing them of fraud; the material had been sold for general salvage, not coastal structural service.\n\nThe supplier replied that afternoon. They had no evidence the pieces had been rated for salt exposure.\n\nIt was a mistake, not a conspiracy.\n\nI could see Maui struggling with that.\n\n\"A villain would be easier,\" he admitted while we waited for the new hardware. \"You fight them. You win. Everyone gets a safe dock.\"\n\n\"Instead we have paperwork.\"\n\n\"An evil thing.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nRenn had another question before we ordered replacements.\n\n\"How do we know the next lot is actually what the label claims?\"\n\nIt was the sort of question I was embarrassed not to have asked. Danse put down the faulty fastener and looked at the supplier's old order sheet.\n\n\"We should have checked the material specification against the conditions at Tideglass,\" he admitted. \"A second invoice isn't a test.\"\n\nTamsin suggested separating the remaining salvaged stock into marked bins: dry interior use, pending verification, and unsafe. Fen wanted the rejected lot traced to its origin without claiming a faulty batch proved malicious intent. Lio wanted to know whether the temporary closure would cost the small boat crews an entire day of work.\n\n\"Not if we schedule the repairs around the safe tide window,\" Fen said. \"But they'll need notice.\"\n\nI wrote the closure times on a draft sign. Edda, who had come to deliver a route report, looked at it and pointed out that the detour described a set of steps without naming an accessible alternative. I added the upper path and asked her to test the directions on the ground before we posted them.\n\nMaui watched the growing list.\n\n\"Is there anything about this nail that doesn't involve a committee?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" Danse said. \"It fails in salt water.\"\n\nToph barked a laugh. So did I.\n\nThe new order listed the actual corrosion rating, the use conditions, and a person responsible for checking the certificate before installation. We set aside money for labor rather than asking the repair crew to donate its afternoon. I wanted the change to look more impressive than a receipt. It didn't.\n\nIt would, however, keep the next person from having to pull rust out of the same board while pretending nobody could have known.\n\nThe replacement fasteners cost more. They had a suitable corrosion rating and came with a maintenance schedule. Mara agreed to cover part of the immediate cost from the repair fund; the remaining expense went to the next council meeting instead of disappearing into someone's unpaid time.\n\nWe worked in daylight. Fen supervised the lift. Lio held a support while the first bracket came free. Maui braced the timber without taking over the job. Danse and Tamsin checked the fit. Toph tested the movement through the stone foundation and reported what she could feel, without pretending she could inspect the metal's internal condition.\n\nWhen the first replacement passed, we cheered.\n\nThen we tested four more.\n\nBy sunset the mooring was usable again at a reduced load. The final load test would happen after the next tide cycle. We posted the restriction at the landing in large letters.\n\nMaui looked at the sign.\n\n\"Does everything here come with conditions?\"\n\n\"Anything worth keeping does,\" Fen said.\n\nI thought about the relationship agreement on our kitchen table. That had conditions too: honesty, choice, the ability to revise. It hadn't made our affection less real.\n\nDanse washed his hands at the work sink.\n\n\"I authorized the first batch for use,\" he said. \"I'll document where I failed to check the environment.\"\n\nTamsin looked at him.\n\n\"I approved the installation.\"\n\n\"And I carried the materials,\" Maui added.\n\nFen nodded. \"Then the report will show what each of you did. Nobody benefits if you turn the record into a competition for blame.\"\n\nRenn wrote the final note. Their handwriting was small and tidy.\n\nAt dinner, the group ate fish soup by the sheltered garden. Danse stayed for one bowl before returning to Brasswatch. Iroh sent tea from Jasmine Steps. Greg arrived late and asked whether the dock was going to make a dramatic noise again.\n\n\"Not if we can help it,\" I said.\n\nHe looked faintly disappointed.\n\nWhen everyone left, Maui held the corroded nail in his palm.\n\n\"We should keep it,\" he said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"So I remember to ask what a thing was made for, not just whether I can lift it.\"\n\nWe set it on the shelf beneath the tide chart, in a little clear jar labeled *Not for the sea.*\n\nMaui leaned toward me.\n\n\"Can I kiss you before you make another label?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe kissed me until I forgot what I'd been about to write. Later I found the pencil under the table and laughed.\n\nThe next morning Fen's report came back: the tide test had passed within the posted limits.\n\nThe jar remained where we could see it.\n\nNot every failure deserved a legend. Some deserved the right replacement part and the humility to read its specification.","word_count":1429},"maui-35":{"number":35,"title":"The Archive's Empty Line","content":"The signature had been scraped away so thoroughly that the paper looked furry.\n\nI leaned toward the glass, then caught myself.\n\n\"Don't breathe on it,\" Severus said from behind me.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"You were certainly preparing to.\"\n\nI stepped back from the burned ledger. The page lay in its padded support beneath a reading lamp, its damaged corner resting on a strip of pale paper. Moonroot Archive smelled of linen, cool plaster, and the sharp tea Severus had refused to let me improve with more sugar.\n\nNeri stood across the table with their hands clasped.\n\n\"The committee wants a name for the public report,\" they said.\n\n\"The committee wants a certainty it doesn't possess,\" Severus answered.\n\n\"People are angry.\"\n\n\"They have reason to be.\"\n\n\"They think we're hiding something.\"\n\nI looked at Neri. \"Are you?\"\n\nThe question stayed in the room.\n\nNeri didn't answer immediately. They had lived with the Closed Bells history longer than I had known this valley. I could see them choosing which memories they would allow into daylight.\n\n\"I remember a keeper who believed closing the paths was an act of protection,\" they said. \"I remember hearing an argument. I don't remember which hand signed this page.\"\n\n\"Then we put that in the report,\" I said.\n\nSeverus nodded once. High praise.\n\nNeri looked miserable. \"It feels insufficient.\"\n\n\"It is insufficient,\" I said. \"People lost their freedom to leave. We don't get to fix that by inventing a culprit.\"\n\nOutside the archive window, the stone arches caught the afternoon sun. Travelers crossed the square carrying baskets and rolled maps, sometimes stopping at the notice board to read the departure rules. The freedom to come and go had become so ordinary that it was hard to remember a time when someone had needed permission.\n\nThat ordinariness was worth protecting.\n\nOrin arrived with a folder of copied invoices. They had been studying the work orders that survived the fire and were, to Severus's delight and everyone else's exhaustion, developing a terrible habit of questioning every adjective.\n\n\"There's a difference between the date the material was ordered and the date it was delivered,\" Orin said, opening the folder. \"Some of the reports treat them as the same day.\"\n\nSeverus reached for the first invoice. \"A dangerous shortcut.\"\n\nOrin beamed.\n\nThe evidence was precise but incomplete. Iron fittings had been ordered before the worst flood reached the valley. The bakery had fed an additional work crew. Fen's copied tide chart showed the lower channel could still have carried water on the day the closure was ordered. But we couldn't tell who signed the order or whether a later instruction had changed its meaning.\n\n\"We need to hear the people affected,\" I said.\n\nNeri's expression tightened. \"Some will not want to speak.\"\n\n\"Then we ask whether they're willing. Privately. We don't make public testimony the price of being believed.\"\n\nSeverus looked at me.\n\n\"That is an important distinction.\"\n\nThe committee meeting was less graceful.\n\nA councilor named Avel stood and demanded to know whether the erased name could be recovered with magic. Severus explained, in a voice thin with patience, that experimental methods risked damaging the original. Another resident suggested using the name of the person who had been keeper at the time, regardless of evidence.\n\nNeri looked as though they might agree just to end the argument.\n\nI stood.\n\n\"Would we accept that if it were our name?\" I asked.\n\nAvel frowned. \"That isn't the same.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\n\"People suffered.\"\n\n\"Yes. Their suffering deserves an accurate record. It doesn't become less real because the signature is blank.\"\n\nA woman at the back rose slowly. \"My grandfather was prevented from leaving. I don't need you to guess who held the pen. I need you to admit the system allowed it.\"\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\nI didn't know her name. I didn't write it down.\n\nNeri stood. Their hands shook.\n\n\"The doors were restricted,\" they said. \"People who wanted to leave were turned away. We have evidence of that. It was wrong.\"\n\nThere was no applause. Some residents looked angry that the statement had taken so long; others looked relieved. Both responses belonged in the room.\n\nThe committee approved an updated report with the empty line preserved, the evidence categories clearly marked, and a voluntary invitation for private testimony. It also funded a review of the present emergency-exit rules, because remembering past harm without changing current procedures would have been a particularly cruel form of nostalgia.\n\nBefore the councilors left, Avel came back to the table, holding the copy of the report as if it might stain his hands.\n\n\"My grandmother was locked out when the bells stopped,\" he said. \"If you won't put a name in that space, what are we supposed to tell her?\"\n\nNeri folded their copy carefully. \"Tell her what the witnesses have said. Tell her what we know happened. Don't tell her we know who signed an order when we don't.\"\n\nAvel shook his head. \"That sounds like hiding.\"\n\nIt did, a little, if I thought about how long families had waited. I was not immune to wanting a single person whose name could carry everybody's anger.\n\n\"Can we separate the facts from the unanswered question on the public notice?\" I asked. \"A blank box with no explanation looks like somebody forgot to finish their work.\"\n\nOrin, the archive clerk, brought out a draft layout. They'd divided the evidence into three sections: recorded closures, witness accounts and documents that needed further review. At the bottom was the scraped signature with the words *identity not established*. Avel pointed to the section where his grandmother's testimony could go.\n\n\"She doesn't want her name out there.\"\n\n\"She shouldn't have to put it out there,\" Neri said.\n\nWe worked out a way for him to carry a blank witness form home. His grandmother could speak, write, decline, or ask a trusted clerk to record only what she chose. There was no deadline for a public accusation and no requirement that she meet the council.\n\nWhen he left, Avel was still angry.\n\nI didn't blame him.\n\nSeverus returned to the reading shelf with a small folded note. \"The original may be examined again after the conservation survey. Not before.\"\n\n\"Do you think they'll ever discover the name?\"\n\n\"I think,\" he said, \"that the answer matters enough not to fabricate.\"\n\nThe practical work felt painfully modest beside what people had lost. Still, Orin had new paper to prepare, a damaged record to protect, and an actual deadline for the council to publish the verified facts. I helped make copies, one by one, until my fingers were gray with dust.\n\nMaui waited for me outside with bread from Mara. He hadn't asked how to get the lost name through strength or magic. He asked if I'd eaten.\n\n\"Ladies and gentlemen,\" Maui whispered, lifting the loaf as though it were a famous trophy, \"the triumphant return of the only man who can solve an ancient mystery with lunch.\"\n\n\"That's not what the archive was doing.\"\n\n\"Exactly. Nobody there thought to bring lunch. Tragic oversight.\"\n\nHis little tattoo offered the bread a tiny salute. I smiled in spite of the ache behind my eyes.\n\nMaui saw me look toward the archive door. His expression shifted. He wanted the scraped name to mean something he could fight. I could almost see the imaginary adversary taking shape in his mind: a villain, a rescue, a clear ending to the legend.\n\n\"Did you find the monster?\" he asked at last.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe nodded, then sat beside me. \"All right. Then tell me what you *did* find. Or don't. I make a fantastic audience, which you should know by now.\"\n\nI tore the loaf in half. He accepted his portion and didn't ask what Severus had learned until I was ready to speak.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then at least one mystery can be solved.\"\n\nI laughed, tired enough that it almost hurt. We ate on the steps without turning somebody else's old suffering into our romantic lesson.\n\nWhen the meeting ended, Severus found me beside the reading shelf.\n\n\"You were very determined,\" he said.\n\n\"So were you.\"\n\n\"An unfortunate family resemblance.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nMaui was waiting outside with a parcel from Fen. He had spent the afternoon checking the upper mooring rather than attending a meeting he had no technical role in. He asked what happened, and I told him as much as the witnesses had agreed could be public.\n\n\"Did you find the person responsible?\" he asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then what did you get?\"\n\n\"A report that tells the truth about what it knows.\"\n\nHe considered that as we walked through the square.\n\n\"I suppose that's better than a very convincing lie.\"\n\n\"Usually.\"\n\nAt home, he set the parcel aside and watched me put the minutes in my notebook.\n\n\"Are you still thinking about the blank line?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nI looked at the green pages, at the places in my own past where memory had refused to give me names.\n\n\"Because I've spent so long wanting an answer that I nearly forgot an answer has to be earned.\"\n\nMaui sat beside me.\n\nHe didn't tell me everything would be all right. He didn't offer to tear open the archive walls.\n\nHe rested his hand beside mine, close enough for me to choose.\n\nI did.\n\nWhen the kitchen grew dark, we lit a lamp and left the notebook open. The empty line remained in the report, not a refusal to care, but a promise not to lie.","word_count":1585},"maui-36":{"number":36,"title":"Permission for Privacy","content":"The chalkboard in Lanternwell Square had acquired a new column.\n\nIt stood beside the bakery menu under the heading *Who's with Jasper?* Someone had drawn six little hearts and written the names of people I cared about, with blank spaces for dates and guesses.\n\nI stared until the letters stopped making sense.\n\nMara came out carrying a tray of rolls. She saw the board and went still.\n\n\"That's not mine.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I was going to wipe it off, but I thought you should decide whether you wanted to see it first.\"\n\nI swallowed. I was furious with the person who'd written it, but I was also strangely ashamed. The chalkboard made my life look like a game people could follow, score, and argue about over breakfast.\n\n\"Would you give me the cloth?\" I asked.\n\nMara handed it over.\n\nI wiped the names away.\n\nBehind me someone said, \"Oh. I thought it was just a joke.\"\n\nI turned. A man I recognized from the market stood holding a basket of apples. He looked embarrassed rather than cruel.\n\n\"Whose joke?\" I asked.\n\n\"People were talking. You and Maui, and the others. We wondered how it all worked.\"\n\n\"Then you could have asked me a question that didn't require exposing six people's private lives.\"\n\nHe shifted the basket. \"I didn't think anyone would mind.\"\n\n\"You didn't ask them.\"\n\nI heard my voice grow sharper. I wanted to make him feel exactly as small as the board had made me feel. I was not proud of that.\n\nMaui arrived before I could say anything else. He looked at the damp board, then at my face.\n\nHis eyes followed the six hearts down the board. I watched the easy grin drain out of his face.\n\n\"Well,\" he said, very softly. \"Whoever drew this has a powerful imagination and absolutely terrible manners.\"\n\nMini Maui came running across his upper arm carrying an imaginary giant eraser. The real Maui looked ready to do the same thing with his hook and perhaps the entire board.\n\nI wanted that anger on my side. I didn't want him to turn it into a public spectacle that would make the story even harder to escape.\n\n\"Jasper,\" he said, voice suddenly big enough to make passersby turn, \"I could give a speech so magnificent they would never gossip again.\"\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\nHe closed his mouth, clearly in pain.\n\n\"Fine. No speech. Extremely difficult, but fine.\"\n\nI saw the impulse hadn't gone anywhere. His fists were tight, and he was watching the man who'd drawn the board. But when I began to speak, he backed up and let people hear me. He wasn't calm. He was choosing not to make his anger more important than mine.\n\n\"What happened?\"\n\nI told him.\n\nHis jaw tightened. For a moment I saw the old Maui, ready to turn the whole square into a battlefield because someone had embarrassed a person he loved.\n\nHe looked at me. \"What do you want?\"\n\n\"Not a speech from you,\" I said quickly. \"Please.\"\n\nHe breathed in, then out. \"All right.\"\n\nI asked Neri for five minutes at the community notice board. A few people gathered, including several who had seen the chalkboard before it was erased. I kept the account plain.\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous,\" I said. \"I prefer polyamory. The people in my relationships know that. They each make their own choices, and some of those choices are private.\"\n\nA woman raised her hand. \"So you don't have to tell everyone?\"\n\n\"No. Neither do you.\"\n\n\"What about fairness?\"\n\n\"Fairness means honesty with the people affected, respecting consent, and keeping the commitments we actually make. It doesn't mean putting everyone's schedule on a public board.\"\n\nSomeone asked whether my partners liked each other.\n\n\"That's their business,\" I said. \"Some have worked together. Some want space. None of them owes the square a performance.\"\n\nThe man with the apples had stayed near the edge of the crowd. He looked down at his basket.\n\nI could have called him out by name.\n\nI didn't.\n\nInstead I asked the council to put a privacy rule beside the public festival guidelines: notice boards were for community business, not speculation about residents' romantic lives. Mara added a second rule about not writing personal contact information without permission.\n\nNeri approved the notice.\n\nIt wasn't a law against gossip. People would still talk. But it established a boundary the community could enforce in the spaces it maintained.\n\nLater, I found Maui at Tideglass repairing a lantern hook.\n\n\"Are you angry?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"At me?\"\n\n\"At the board.\"\n\nI leaned against the post. \"You didn't say much.\"\n\n\"You asked me not to.\"\n\n\"Was that difficult?\"\n\n\"Extremely. I had three speeches ready.\"\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nHe put down the hook.\n\n\"Did you mean what you said about us not needing to explain everything?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because sometimes I don't want to know what you and Severus talk about, or how Iroh says goodnight. Sometimes I do want to know when your plans affect ours. And sometimes I worry that wanting that makes me selfish.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"You can ask. I can decide what belongs to me to share. If it belongs to someone else too, I ask them first.\"\n\nHe frowned.\n\n\"What about kisses?\"\n\n\"Those are ours when they're between us. We can talk about them if we both want. They're not the village's property.\"\n\nHe smiled slowly.\n\nMara came back across the square with a fresh price board, still looking upset about what someone had written on the chalk.\n\n\"I was thinking,\" she said. \"Maybe the notice boards should be separated. People keep putting personal messages beside the public safety information because it's the only place everybody knows to look.\"\n\nNeri, who had been updating the departure instructions, stopped to listen.\n\n\"A public board for services,\" they said. \"A private message shelf for things addressed to one person. The shelf would need a way to refuse unwanted notes, too.\"\n\n\"And no list of who collects what,\" I added.\n\nMara nodded. \"I don't want the bakery becoming a gossip desk.\"\n\nWe worked out a small plan that afternoon. The service board would hold prices, closures, community meeting times and voluntarily credited work. Personal messages could be left in sealed envelopes through the registrar, who could return anything unwanted without posting it in public.\n\nPip offered to paint a sign. Edda made them check that the new shelf could be reached without blocking the walkway. A new rule wasn't finished just because someone had written it in attractive letters.\n\nMaui held the ladder while the sign went up.\n\n\"I could give a speech about everyone's freedom,\" he whispered.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Would it be bad?\"\n\n\"Probably magnificent.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"And unnecessary.\"\n\nHe sighed theatrically, but let Pip fasten the last corner. When the square was tidy again, he reached for my hand and waited.\n\n\"I like that you can tell me what you want private,\" he said.\n\n\"Even when you want to know?\"\n\n\"Especially then. I may still be curious.\"\n\n\"I'll still tell you the things that affect us. You don't need a public scoreboard to know I care.\"\n\nWe stood beneath the new sign while people walked past without noticing us. It was wonderfully ordinary. The next time somebody wanted to announce who Jasper had kissed, the town at least had a clear reason to say that wasn't what the public board was for.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThe sunset lit the signal post in amber. I could hear Lio arguing with Fen about a skiff measurement and the bakery bell sounding faintly from the square. There was a whole life around us, full of people who deserved to have some parts remain their own.\n\nMaui stepped closer.\n\n\"May I kiss you somewhere nobody's taking notes?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe went home by the upper path, laughing when the wind tried to steal his cloak. At the door he kissed me, then again after I pulled him closer. The rest of the evening was private, the door closed because we had chosen it.\n\nBefore we left, Neri caught us beside the fountain.\n\n\"I have a problem,\" they said, carrying a second sheet of paper. \"We keep an arrival book so people can be found when crossings go wrong. If we stop taking names, we lose the safety record. But if we publish who went where—\"\n\n\"We do the chalkboard all over again,\" I finished.\n\nEdda, passing toward the archive, stopped to listen. She pointed out that the registrar's desk couldn't be reached without stairs after the side path flooded. That made an emergency contact fairly useless if the person who needed the record couldn't get inside.\n\nWe wrote a new rule together. The public board would show Threadway closures and available services, not visitors' destinations. A traveler could give Neri a restricted emergency contact, refuse to list an unrelated private appointment, and request correction without broadcasting their business. If someone challenged an entry, a second registrar would review it.\n\nMaui studied the page. \"That sounds like a lot of work just to stop people being nosy.\"\n\n\"It's work we should have done before the board hurt somebody,\" I said.\n\nHe didn't argue. Instead he held the ladder while Neri pinned the notice in a place Edda could actually read.\n\nThe man with the apples approached again.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" he said. \"I made it a joke because I didn't think about the people underneath it.\"\n\nI was still angry. \"Don't do it again.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\nI wasn't required to make him feel better. He went back to his market stall, and I went home with Maui.\n\nOn the path, Maui touched my wrist. \"Ask about the ledger tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Ask about your other partners?\"\n\n\"Only what affects our agreements, like we said.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I am becoming a very sophisticated man.\"\n\n\"You held a ladder.\"\n\n\"Heroically.\"\n\nI kissed his cheek, which managed to shut him up for nearly ten seconds.\n\n\nThe next morning the chalkboard held the day's bread prices and the tide warning, nothing else.\n\nThere were six hearts still faintly visible beneath the damp surface.\n\nI didn't erase them again.\n\nThe boundary was the important part. The trace of somebody's mistake could stay without deciding what happened next.","word_count":1707},"maui-37":{"number":37,"title":"The Tide Between Us","content":"Vel was sitting at our kitchen table when I came home.\n\nI recognized the small wooden fish beside her cup. It was the one she'd returned after Maui refused to shame Tomas for leaving Lanternweald. I hadn't seen her since.\n\nMaui stood at the stove looking uncomfortable.\n\nHe'd made too much tea. There were three cups on the table and a fourth set out for somebody who hadn't arrived. The scene was so obviously a hasty effort at hospitality that I almost laughed.\n\n\"Before you start,\" he told me, \"there's an explanation.\"\n\n\"Oh, good. I love those.\"\n\n\"I rescued a stranded traveler, arranged a comfortable room, and made an excellent pot of tea. Heroic work.\"\n\n\"Did you ask the other person who lives here?\"\n\nThe enormous grin slid off his face.\n\n\"That's the part of the story I'm hoping to revise.\"\n\nMini Maui, apparently an unhelpful editor, covered his tiny eyes. Maui scowled down at the tattoo and looked back at me.\n\nVel was watching us with an expression I couldn't read. I didn't want him to turn her need for a safe place into an apology performed for my benefit.\n\n\"Let her tell us what she needs,\" I said.\n\n\"Right.\" His voice softened. \"I'm listening.\"\n\nHe still looked miserable, and I knew he wanted to be forgiven immediately. He made himself wait while Vel explained the broken cart. In that small delay, he behaved more bravely than any of his speeches could have managed.\n\n\"Jasper,\" he said, \"Vel needs a place to stay until the north road clears.\"\n\nI looked from him to her.\n\n\"Does she?\"\n\nVel lifted her chin. \"My cart axle broke. The shelter is full.\"\n\n\"The shelter isn't full,\" I said, because I'd helped with the count that afternoon. \"There are rooms available on the upper level.\"\n\nMaui shot me a warning glance.\n\nVel looked wounded.\n\nI put down my bag.\n\n\"Can we talk outside?\" I asked Maui.\n\nHe followed me to the porch. The sea wind was strong enough to make the lantern shiver on its hook.\n\n\"You brought someone into our home without asking,\" I said.\n\n\"She needed help.\"\n\n\"Yes. She deserves help. That's not the same as deciding she can sleep here.\"\n\n\"She's had a hard day.\"\n\n\"So have I.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You're being harsh.\"\n\nI felt something hot and unpleasant rise behind my ribs.\n\n\"And you're being generous with space that isn't only yours.\"\n\nHis face changed. We stood very still.\n\nThen the door opened and Vel stepped onto the porch.\n\n\"I can hear you,\" she said.\n\nI closed my eyes.\n\n\"Vel, I'm sorry. You shouldn't have had to listen to us argue over your head.\"\n\nShe looked from me to Maui.\n\n\"I thought he said I could stay.\"\n\n\"He did,\" Maui answered quietly. \"Without checking with Jasper first. That's my mistake.\"\n\nVel's mouth tightened.\n\n\"Then I'll go.\"\n\n\"Not into the rain,\" I said. \"There's a room at the hall. I can walk you there and help with the cart arrangements tomorrow, if you'd like.\"\n\nShe stared at me.\n\n\"I don't want charity.\"\n\n\"Then we can talk about the actual cost of the room. The hall has a posted rate for people who can pay, and emergency funds for people who can't. You decide what you want to share.\"\n\nVel looked away.\n\nAfter a moment she nodded.\n\nMaui fetched her bag. He offered to escort her, but she asked me instead. The choice seemed to hurt him; he didn't argue.\n\nWe walked to the hall along the safe ridge. Vel spoke only once.\n\n\"I thought if I came back, he'd forgive me for being angry.\"\n\n\"He might,\" I said. \"But you don't have to stay in his house to talk to him.\"\n\nShe looked at me sharply.\n\n\"I didn't want to make trouble.\"\n\n\"I believe you.\"\n\nWe checked the room. It was clean, dry, and had a working lamp. A volunteer explained how the door locked from the inside. Vel accepted the key and asked whether Mara would be selling bread in the morning.\n\n\"She will.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nWhen I returned home, Maui was washing the cups. He'd scrubbed the table, too, as though the wood itself had taken part in the argument.\n\n\"She's safe?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI took off my coat.\n\n\"I don't want to fight,\" I said.\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\n\"Then we have to talk about what happened.\"\n\nHe set down the cloth.\n\n\"I wanted to prove I could still help her. After Tomas left, she stopped believing in me. I thought if I gave her a place to stay, she might understand.\"\n\n\"That's not your house to give alone.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nI sat across from him.\n\n\"Your kindness became an unasked favor from me. And when I objected, you made me sound cruel.\"\n\nHe looked stricken.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nI held up a hand. \"I wasn't fair either. I corrected Vel in front of you both instead of asking what she needed. I treated her like a problem before I'd even listened.\"\n\nMaui exhaled.\n\n\"We were trying to be good people.\"\n\n\"We were trying to look like good people,\" I said. \"At least a little.\"\n\nHe gave a rueful laugh.\n\nI thought of Vel's expression when she realized Maui's invitation hadn't included me. She had come in from bad weather, tired and angry, and had been made to feel like a problem two people were arguing about.\n\n\"There's something else,\" I said.\n\nMaui waited.\n\n\"The hall isn't a punishment. And we should stop talking as if shelter is a prize that goes to people who make us feel like heroes.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. \"I know. I panicked when she said she needed somewhere safe.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow, ask the hall coordinator what the actual emergency-housing procedure is. Don't ask for Vel's private notes. Ask what we can do when someone comes to our door.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n\"We could keep a list of places with space and safe routes,\" he said.\n\n\"With the coordinator's permission. And it needs to be kept current.\"\n\nMaui found the notebook beneath the calendar and wrote *Ask first. Check capacity. Don't promise another person's room.* His handwriting took up almost an entire page.\n\n\"You're making a law for our kitchen,\" he observed.\n\n\"Not a law. A boundary we can both understand when we're frightened.\"\n\n\"What if you aren't home?\"\n\n\"Help with an immediate safety need. Give someone warmth, water, a way to contact a registrar. But don't assign my bed or somebody else's privacy because you want to feel useful.\"\n\nHe looked at the empty chair where Vel had sat.\n\n\"I didn't let her choose either,\" he said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"I'll apologize to her for that. If she wants to hear it.\"\n\nWe sat together until the kettle stopped hissing. Then he wrote a second line in smaller letters: *An apology isn't a ticket back inside.* I didn't tell him how much it mattered that he'd thought of that himself.\n\nThat was the beginning of the repair, not the end.\n\nWe talked about shared space. We agreed that either of us could offer immediate help in a true emergency, but an overnight guest required discussion when both of us were available. If someone needed shelter and our answer was no, we would help them find an alternative rather than leave them without options.\n\nMaui wrote it beneath our calendar agreement, in the same enormous handwriting.\n\nThen he stared at the page.\n\n\"Do you still want to live with me?\"\n\nI looked at the sink full of cups and the crooked fish drawing beside the stove.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"But wanting doesn't remove the work.\"\n\nHe came around the table, slowly enough for me to choose whether to meet him.\n\n\"Can I hold you?\"\n\nI stood.\n\nHe wrapped his arms around me. His breath shook once against my hair.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" he whispered.\n\n\"So am I.\"\n\nI lifted my face. His kiss was gentle, then warmer when I drew him closer. There were no fireworks, no perfect words, and no audience. Later we closed the door and left the rest of the night between us.\n\nIn the morning, Maui went to the hall.\n\nHe asked Vel whether she wanted to talk. She told him not yet.\n\nHe accepted it.\n\nHe returned with bread from Mara, a message that Vel's cart would be repaired after the morning inspection, and the uncomfortable realization that apology didn't entitle him to immediate forgiveness.\n\nWe ate breakfast together.\n\nThe tide was coming in.\n\nFor once, neither of us mistook that movement for a reason to hurry.","word_count":1428},"maui-38":{"number":38,"title":"A Bridge with Too Many Owners","content":"The bridge had three names and no maintenance budget.\n\nIn Tideglass we called it the canal crossing. People from Riverstone called it the east span. Brasswatch's invoices referred to it as Structural Link R-4, which made Toph laugh so hard she nearly fell off the bench.\n\n\"Wonderful,\" she said. \"If it collapses, we can file a complaint with the alphabet.\"\n\nTamsin did not laugh. She had the inspection report spread out in front of her, and the numbers were unpleasant.\n\nThe eastern handrail needed replacement. The expansion joint had swollen after the last flood. A support near Sela's vegetable garden still required monitoring. Riverstone had paid for most of the original construction, but Tideglass used the crossing to bring fish and coastal herbs to market. Brasswatch's carts brought tools over it. Jasmine Steps depended on it when the upper delivery road was closed.\n\nEverybody used the bridge.\n\nNobody had agreed who would pay to keep it safe.\n\nAang studied the report. Katara stood beside him with the water delivery schedule. Toph had both feet on the ground, listening to movement through the old stone foundation. Edda sat near the door, positioned where she could see the drawings without asking someone to hold them up.\n\nMaui leaned toward me.\n\n\"I could repair the rail.\"\n\n\"You could,\" I whispered. \"Then who checks it next year?\"\n\nHe sighed.\n\nThat was the question that ruined every easy solution.\n\nThe committee meeting began with a disagreement about jurisdiction. A Riverstone representative said that a bridge built with local funds belonged to Riverstone. Fen replied that tidal water carried the risk from Tideglass. Danse argued that anyone sending heavy carts over the span should contribute to the wear. A market vendor protested that they could barely afford transport fees already.\n\nSela stood up.\n\n\"My garden flooded because someone moved a cart path without checking the drainage. I don't care who owns the bridge on paper. I want the next repair to include the water under it.\"\n\nToph nodded.\n\n\"Finally, a person asking about the ground.\"\n\nAang tried to settle the room. He had a calm, earnest voice, and people liked listening to the Avatar. I could see him beginning to offer a neat compromise before the affected residents had finished speaking.\n\nKatara touched his arm.\n\n\"Let them finish.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nIt was a small thing, and it changed the meeting.\n\nEdda described how the alternate route became too steep after rain. A cart driver explained why heavier loads were being diverted to the ridge, increasing delivery times. Mara laid out the pantry's costs. Fen showed tide marks from the last storm. Nobody had the whole answer.\n\nI took notes, then realized I was writing the same word on every page.\n\n*Owner.*\n\nI crossed it out and wrote *responsibility.*\n\nWhen the discussion broke for lunch, Maui found me beside the notice board.\n\n\"Are they going to argue all day?\"\n\n\"Probably.\"\n\n\"What if I pay for the rail myself?\"\n\n\"That would pay for the rail.\"\n\n\"It would help.\"\n\n\"Yes. But then everyone would wait for you the next time it broke.\"\n\nHe looked toward the bridge.\n\n\"I don't want that.\"\n\n\"Then help them make a plan they can keep without you.\"\n\nWe returned after lunch.\n\nI asked whether the committee would consider a shared maintenance ledger. It would record which districts used the crossing, what loads passed over it, what inspections were due, and what each district could contribute without pretending their resources were equal. Urgent repairs would be funded from a shared reserve, but the reserve's rules would be public.\n\nThe idea was not greeted with enthusiasm.\n\n\"More paperwork,\" Toph said.\n\n\"More accurate paperwork,\" I answered.\n\n\"Still paperwork.\"\n\nDanse smiled faintly.\n\nWe spent two hours negotiating what counted as a fair contribution. The final proposal did not divide the cost equally by district. The market and pantry would pay lower fees for essential deliveries, while heavier commercial transport contributed more toward wear. Riverstone would maintain the public approach. Brasswatch would provide scheduled technical inspections, with workers paid. Tideglass would monitor flood and salt exposure. The council would review the arrangement after the first storm season.\n\nNobody got everything they wanted.\n\nThat was probably why it was worth trying.\n\nWhen the vote came, the proposal passed narrowly.\n\nThe bridge remained closed until the repairs and inspections were complete.\n\nI felt disappointed, though I had argued for the restriction myself. People needed the crossing now. The idea that being responsible could still mean waiting was hard to bear.\n\nWhen the meeting finally broke, Edda asked whether we would walk the approach before calling the new budget fair.\n\n\"The ledger assumes a delivery cart can use the upper path,\" she said. \"It can't, if the curb is still there.\"\n\nMaui looked toward the bridge. \"I could lift the cart over.\"\n\nToph's head turned toward him.\n\n\"Wonderful. We'll put a big sign on the bridge: WAIT FOR THE DEMIGOD.\"\n\n\"That sounds like an excellent sign.\"\n\n\"Your head wouldn't fit on it.\"\n\nMaui threw back his shoulders. \"We could build a larger sign.\"\n\nI groaned. He grinned at me, thoroughly pleased with himself.\n\nEdda tapped the low curb with her cane. \"The bridge has to work for people who aren't carried by somebody famous.\"\n\nThe grin held for a beat too long. He understood before he wanted to. Being strong enough to lift a cart was not the same as having a solution that belonged to the town.\n\nMini Maui produced a tiny imaginary cart and hoisted it triumphantly overhead. Then he set it down before a curb that stopped it dead.\n\n\"All right,\" Maui said. \"You win, little nightmare.\"\n\n\"Which one of us?\" Toph asked.\n\n\"All of you. A stunning defeat.\"\n\nHe offered to move the old stone curb only after the engineers agreed on a new approach. Toph made a noise of approval that sounded suspiciously like an insult. Maui beamed as if he'd received a medal.\n\n\"Once,\" Edda answered. \"Who does it when you aren't here?\"\n\nThat question got through to him faster than any speech I might have made. He rubbed the back of his neck, glanced at the rail he had volunteered to fix, and asked Edda to show him where the problem began.\n\nThe curb was only a few inches high. It was also enough to send a loaded handcart tipping sideways. Edda explained why a painted arrow was not a substitute for a level route. Lio arrived with a small crate to demonstrate where the wheel caught. Toph stood at the joint and felt the vibration as he tried it.\n\n\"Don't force it,\" she said. \"You'll just teach the wheel to fall over.\"\n\nTamsin took the measurements. The repair wasn't covered by the bridge fund as written. It belonged to the connecting street, which the council had left out of the budget because the drawing ended at the bridge's edge.\n\n\"Of course it does,\" I muttered.\n\nWe went back inside before anybody could declare victory. The committee added the street approach to the work order and paid for a second accessibility assessment. The new estimate would come to the public meeting before anyone reopened the span. Aang offered to help carry equipment at the inspection, but he didn't decide the grade of the ramp for the people who would use it.\n\nBy the time we left, the sun had shifted behind the market roofs. Maui was quiet.\n\n\"That would have been easier if I could just put the cart over,\" he said.\n\n\"For the cart today?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And for the next ten years?\"\n\nHe made a face. \"You have become unreasonable about future carts.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHe brushed damp hair off my forehead and stopped with his hand hovering at my cheek.\n\n\"May I?\"\n\nI leaned into his palm. \"You may.\"\n\nHis thumb moved once along my skin. It should have been an ordinary touch, but I could still feel the heat of the meeting in my chest, and I was grateful he'd asked me something that didn't need a budget vote.\n\n\"Will you let me buy you supper?\" he asked.\n\n\"With our actual supper money?\"\n\n\"I am trying to become a respectable citizen.\"\n\nI laughed. We went to the market, bought food we could afford, and left the bridge closed until the people responsible had finished the work.\n\nAfter the meeting, Toph came to find me.\n\n\"You looked like you swallowed a bucket of sand,\" she said.\n\n\"That was roughly how the vote felt.\"\n\n\"You got your ledger.\"\n\n\"We got a process. The bridge is still closed.\"\n\nShe tapped her heel against the floor.\n\n\"Stone doesn't care if the vote was popular. It cares whether the support holds.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That sounds like you.\"\n\n\"It is me.\"\n\nMaui joined us carrying two cups of tea. He handed one to Toph.\n\nShe sniffed it.\n\n\"From Iroh?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then you're forgiven for whatever you did before lunch.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nWe walked toward the bridge at dusk. The closure signs glowed beneath the lanterns, and the water moved through its temporary channel. Aang and Katara were still discussing the delivery schedule nearby. Edda checked the path with Pip before turning toward home.\n\nMaui slipped his arm around my shoulders.\n\n\"You're tired.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\n\"Can I make you dinner?\"\n\n\"Only if you promise not to rebuild the kitchen.\"\n\n\"I promise nothing.\"\n\nI looked up at him.\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"Fine. I promise.\"\n\nWe walked home as the first evening tide touched the low stones. The bridge had too many names, perhaps, but its responsibilities were finally written down.\n\nThat was a beginning.\n\nNot a magnificent one.\n\nA useful one.","word_count":1599},"maui-39":{"number":39,"title":"The Keeper's Refusal","content":"Neri told us the truth without looking at either of us.\n\nThey sat in the archive's west room, where the burned ledger rested under protective cloth. The door was closed, but not locked. Severus had offered to stay and had accepted Neri's request that he wait outside.\n\nIt was the first time Neri had asked me to hear something without calling it a meeting.\n\nMaui sat beside me. He'd come because I wanted company, not because the old keepers belonged to his legend.\n\nNeri folded their hands.\n\n\"I watched someone turn a traveler away during the Closed Bells,\" they said.\n\nThe room seemed to tighten.\n\n\"How old were you?\" I asked.\n\n\"Old enough to understand the words. Young enough to believe an official would know better than I did.\"\n\nThey looked at the ledger.\n\n\"The keeper said the channels were unstable. He said opening the door might endanger everyone. A woman wanted to leave for her homeworld. She had family waiting. She begged him to let her pass.\"\n\n\"Did he?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nI heard the sea outside, impossibly distant from Moonroot Archive.\n\n\"What happened to her?\" I asked.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nNeri's voice broke on the last word.\n\nThey took a breath.\n\n\"The keeper ordered the arch sealed. He used the language of safety and protection. He spoke as if refusing her was an act of compassion. I stood there and did nothing.\"\n\nMaui's hand tightened on his knee.\n\nI wanted to say that Neri had been a child, that they hadn't been responsible, that remembering was enough. But the woman had still been turned away. Her family had still waited on the other side.\n\n\"I don't know what you could have done then,\" I said. \"I do know what you can do now.\"\n\nNeri looked up.\n\n\"Tell the truth in the record,\" I said. \"Help us change the rules that allowed it. And don't ask the people affected to make you feel forgiven.\"\n\nThey looked as though I had struck them.\n\n\"I wasn't going to—\"\n\n\"I know,\" I said softly. \"But I think you've been carrying this alone because you wanted a version where your silence made sense.\"\n\nThey stared at the table.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe word was almost inaudible.\n\nWe stayed a while. I asked what Neri remembered about the keeper's instructions, the weather, and the public notice. I did not ask for a name Neri had already said they couldn't reliably provide. Their memory could be a witness to an action without being proof of the scratched signature in the ledger.\n\nMaui listened.\n\nAt one point Neri said, \"I thought he was trying to keep us safe.\"\n\nMaui shifted in his chair.\n\n\"I've told people I was protecting them,\" he said. \"Sometimes I was. Sometimes I was just frightened of being blamed if something went wrong.\"\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"What did you do?\"\n\n\"Things I regret.\"\n\nHe didn't turn the answer into a story about his greatness.\n\nLater, Severus returned and asked whether Neri wanted their account documented as a signed statement, an anonymous summary, or kept private while they considered it. They chose a signed statement with restricted access until the wording could be reviewed.\n\nOrin prepared the form.\n\nI stood beside Neri while they read it.\n\nThe final paragraph did not identify the keeper by name. It recorded what Neri had witnessed: a traveler requesting departure, an official refusing it, and a claimed safety reason that had not been documented with an alternative plan.\n\nNeri signed.\n\nThen they put down the pen and wept.\n\nI did not know whether to embrace them. I asked.\n\nThey nodded.\n\nI held them for a moment, feeling how thin their shoulders were beneath the old coat. My own throat hurt. I wanted the world to be the kind of place where admitting wrong made the consequence disappear.\n\nIt wasn't.\n\nTwo days passed before Neri asked Orin for a second copy of the testimony.\n\nWe were in Moonroot's sorting room, where scraps of damaged paper waited in shallow trays. Orin had labeled each tray by the document it came from, not by the conclusion somebody hoped it would support.\n\n\"I've been reading what I signed,\" Neri said. \"I didn't remember whether the traveler was alone.\"\n\nOrin stopped writing. \"Then we can amend the statement.\"\n\n\"Would that make the whole thing unreliable?\"\n\n\"It would make the amendment visible,\" Orin said. \"That's different.\"\n\nNeri sat down. I could see their hands shaking around the edge of the chair. Maui brought a cup of water and set it nearby without making them take it.\n\n\"I kept seeing the child's red sleeve,\" Neri said. \"The traveler might have been holding their hand. Or the child might have stood beside the official. I can't make the memory settle.\"\n\nNobody offered to tell them which version sounded more likely.\n\nOrin recorded the uncertainty in an addendum and preserved the original wording alongside it. Neri could still choose whether the statement was read publicly. The restricted copy would keep both versions, with the date of the correction and the reason for it.\n\n\"I thought remembering would make me feel less frightened,\" Neri admitted.\n\n\"Sometimes remembering gives you more than you know how to use,\" I said.\n\nMaui sat on the other side of the table. \"When I tell a story about something I did badly, I always want it to become the part where I learned to do better.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb along the curve of his hook before speaking again.\n\n\"I used to make every story end with somebody cheering,\" he said. \"Catch a monster, save a village, cue the applause. You leave out the parts where you're frightened, or where you were the fool who caused the trouble.\"\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"You can tell those parts?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\" His grin was quick and painful. \"Depends who's listening. The tattoos are brutal critics.\"\n\nMini Maui popped up and pointed at a dark little image farther across his chest, one Maui rarely called attention to. He covered it with his hand. I wasn't going to ask him to turn his history into entertainment for this meeting.\n\n\"The missing line matters,\" he told Neri. \"I'm good at making stories sound wonderful. Doesn't make every part true.\"\n\nIt was more honest than one of his polished lessons. His pride hadn't disappeared. Neither had his guilt. He could still be a storyteller without making the archive's evidence follow his preferred ending.\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"Did you?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. And sometimes I only learned I should have listened earlier.\"\n\nThe answer made Neri breathe a little more easily.\n\nBefore we left, Orin showed us where the public copy would omit the child's details. The testimony was about a forced refusal of passage. Nobody needed to turn a half-remembered child into a clue for strangers to hunt.\n\nOn the walk to the square, Maui reached for my hand. I accepted, even though I was still thinking about the scarred page and Neri's face when they said they weren't sure.\n\n\"You're allowed not to know everything,\" he said.\n\n\"So are you.\"\n\n\"Terrible rule. It spoils all my dramatic speeches.\"\n\n\"Maybe one or two of them will survive.\"\n\nHe grinned. It felt good to laugh without making the day feel smaller.\n\nAt the next council session, Neri gave permission to read the verified portion of their testimony. The room listened. A woman in the audience asked whether other witnesses could be contacted without being pressured to relive what had happened.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri said. \"And they may refuse.\"\n\nSomeone asked whether the old keeper had meant well.\n\nI could feel the room searching for an easier ending.\n\nI stood.\n\n\"Intention matters to understanding how decisions were made,\" I said. \"It doesn't make a locked exit harmless. A person who wasn't allowed to leave was still denied a choice.\"\n\nThe council approved a review of every emergency crossing procedure. Volunteers would be trained to explain safe options and record only the information necessary to help. The lower service passage would stay closed until the structural team could inspect it; closure of an unsafe maintenance tunnel would never be used to justify blocking the independent traveler Threadways.\n\nThat distinction took an entire afternoon to write correctly.\n\nAfterward, Maui and I walked to the cove.\n\n\"I wanted to shout,\" he admitted.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You didn't.\"\n\n\"I wanted to.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Why didn't you?\"\n\n\"Because Neri wasn't asking me to make them feel good. They were trying to say something difficult. I wanted to hear it without turning their shame into my performance.\"\n\nMaui nodded.\n\nThe light had grown gold over the sea-glass orchard. I could smell wet earth and the faint spice of rosemary. The little bench stood empty.\n\nWe sat.\n\n\"I was wrong about many things,\" Maui said. \"Still am, probably.\"\n\n\"Very probably.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"Do you think Neri's testimony will help?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. Not because it ends the question. Because people can now ask better questions.\"\n\nHe put his hand over mine.\n\nWe watched a boat make the safe turn toward the upper landing, its lantern moving steadily through the evening light.\n\nThe harbor did not become innocent because somebody had spoken.\n\nBut the words were out in the open.\n\nThat mattered.","word_count":1545},"maui-40":{"number":40,"title":"A Day of Ordinary Things","content":"Maui ruined the laundry.\n\nNot all of it. Just my favorite shirt, which had once been blue and now looked like something rescued from the bottom of a teapot.\n\nI held it up between two fingers.\n\n\"What happened?\"\n\nHe glanced at the washing tub.\n\n\"Alchemical incident.\"\n\n\"Did you put the red cloth in with everything else?\"\n\n\"It was a small red cloth.\"\n\n\"That isn't a defense.\"\n\n\"It was very small.\"\n\nI started laughing. I was too tired to be properly angry, and the sight of the mighty Maui trying to explain how a single handkerchief had defeated him was impossible to resist.\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\n\"You're not furious?\"\n\n\"I liked that shirt.\"\n\n\"I'll get you another.\"\n\n\"Maybe. But I want to know how to avoid this happening again.\"\n\nHe looked toward the washing line.\n\n\"Separate the red cloth?\"\n\n\"Now you're learning.\"\n\nThe day had been set aside for doing nothing useful.\n\nWe were terrible at it.\n\nMaui began by deciding the kitchen shelves needed rearranging. I found three misplaced cups, two jars of rosemary, and a spoon that had apparently been living behind the flour for several weeks. Then I offered to measure a new hook for the pantry door and wrote down the wrong number twice.\n\nMara saw the sketch.\n\n\"Are you building a hook for a giant?\" she asked.\n\n\"Possibly.\"\n\nShe held the ruler against the actual door and smiled.\n\n\"Try again.\"\n\nI did.\n\nWe returned to Tideglass with the corrected measurement and a bag of bread. On the way, Lio called from the skiff, where the new seat needed sanding.\n\nMaui took one step toward the landing.\n\nI caught his hand.\n\n\"Not today.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n\"I was only going to look.\"\n\n\"You were going to fix.\"\n\n\"Perhaps.\"\n\n\"Lio has Fen.\"\n\nHe sighed dramatically, then waved instead.\n\nLio waved back and returned to the work.\n\nWe had lunch on the porch, where the wind moved through the hanging lantern hooks and the sea made its ordinary music below. Maui told me a story from his homeworld that did not involve a triumph. It involved an elderly fisherman, a stubborn basket, and a lesson about not borrowing tools without returning them.\n\n\"For the record,\" Maui said, holding up the crooked wooden spoon he'd been using, \"the fisherman owed me that basket. It was a very good basket. Everyone knows you can't simply abandon a good basket.\"\n\n\"You borrowed it without asking.\"\n\n\"A minor technicality.\"\n\n\"And you broke it.\"\n\n\"An unexpected stress test.\"\n\nI laughed. Mini Maui drew a little fish beside the spoon in the tattoos and crossed its arms as if it were the offended fisherman.\n\n\"Even my own skin is against me,\" he complained.\n\n\"Did you replace it?\"\n\n\"Eventually. With a better one. And a spectacular apology.\"\n\n\"How spectacular?\"\n\nHe considered. \"There may have been a canoe involved.\"\n\nI could tell he was editing the story for effect, as usual. The real apology had probably been a quiet visit and a new basket, perhaps accompanied by one terrible joke. I loved the impossible boasting and the man beneath it, who still seemed surprised when I wanted to hear about a failure rather than a triumph. That afternoon he would make another error with a shutter. I suspected we'd hear the story of that disaster for years.\n\n\"Did you return the basket?\" I asked.\n\n\"Eventually.\"\n\n\"Before or after you broke it?\"\n\nHe stared into his soup.\n\nI laughed again.\n\nIn the afternoon we tried to repair the loose shutter by the bedroom window. I measured the hinge spacing incorrectly. Maui drilled the first hole too close to the edge. We stood looking at the damage.\n\n\"Well,\" he said.\n\n\"Well.\"\n\n\"I could make a new shutter.\"\n\n\"Or we could ask Tamsin what kind of patch will hold.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nWe wrote a note rather than pretending the mistake hadn't happened.\n\nAt dusk, I made dinner. The soup was too salty. The bread had cooled. Maui burned his fingers trying to take a pan off the stove without a cloth.\n\nI put a cool wet towel in his hand.\n\n\"You're a disaster,\" I told him.\n\n\"Yet you're still here.\"\n\n\"Very concerning, isn't it?\"\n\nHe smiled, but a small hesitation crossed his face.\n\nI set down the towel.\n\n\"What?\"\n\nHe looked away.\n\n\"Sometimes I think you're going to get tired of how often I need to learn things that everyone else already knows.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"I learn things all the time.\"\n\n\"You don't make them into public disasters.\"\n\n\"Ask Mara about the hook measurement.\"\n\n\"That's different.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nHe struggled for an answer.\n\nI watched the kitchen light move over his face. He had spent so long being expected to save people that the ordinary incompetence of living with someone seemed almost unbearable.\n\n\"Listen,\" I said. \"I don't need you to be perfect. I need you to admit when something went wrong, help repair it, and let me do the same.\"\n\nHe looked at the pink shirt hanging by the stove.\n\n\"Even when the laundry is a tragedy?\"\n\n\"Especially then.\"\n\nAfter supper, Maui asked whether the shirt could be saved.\n\n\"Maybe,\" I said. \"But I don't want you to make it your personal mission.\"\n\nHe looked down at the pink fabric drying by the stove. \"What if I want to try?\"\n\n\"Then ask me before you experiment on my clothes again.\"\n\nHe considered that. \"Fair.\"\n\nWe decided to ask the cloth worker at the market whether the color could be reduced without damaging the seams. It might cost more than replacing the shirt; it might be impossible. I wanted to make the choice rather than wake to discover he'd taken it somewhere without asking.\n\nMaui folded the shirt separately from the rest of the washing and checked the labels on the next load. I pretended not to watch.\n\n\"I appreciate that you listened after I was upset,\" I said. \"You didn't make the mistake disappear. You're changing what you do next.\"\n\nHe looked pleased, though he tried to hide it. \"Is that romance?\"\n\n\"It can be.\"\n\n\"I have been doing romance all wrong.\"\n\n\"Frequently.\"\n\nHe grinned, dried his hands and looked at the shirt by the stove. We still had chores to finish, and he had not tried to turn the evening into an elaborate apology. I liked that much better than a surprise trip to the market.\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"Can I keep trying?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe leaned toward me, slow enough for me to meet him. I kissed him, tasting the faint edge of burnt bread and hearing his laugh when I pulled away.\n\n\"Romantic,\" he murmured.\n\n\"Ridiculous.\"\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nBefore we ate, I found the household notebook buried beneath a bowl of fruit. It had shopping lists, wrong measurements and the repairs we'd decided not to try without help.\n\nMaui eyed the page. \"The shirt is going in there, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Under catastrophic losses.\"\n\n\"Put down that I bravely attempted laundry.\"\n\n\"I'll draw you a medal.\"\n\nHe leaned across the table to draw one himself, and his arm brushed mine. It was difficult to remember I was annoyed while his cheek was so close. I caught the pen before he could illustrate the entire war against the red handkerchief.\n\n\"Would it help if I collected bread tomorrow?\" he asked.\n\nI blinked. He was learning that helping didn't always mean taking over a broken bridge.\n\n\"I'd like that,\" I said. \"But ask Mara's price before offering to trade her a heroic story.\"\n\n\"I only tried that once.\"\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\n\"She was merciless.\"\n\nWe divided the next day's work: bread for him, curtain mending for me, shutter inspection for Tamsin. Lio knocked to return the sanding block, took one look at the pink shirt and visibly struggled not to laugh.\n\n\"Don't,\" I warned him.\n\n\"I wasn't going to say a word.\"\n\n\"Your face said several,\" Maui told him.\n\nWhen Lio left, I set the notebook aside. Maui moved closer, pausing long enough for me to decide whether I wanted to meet him. I did. We kissed, his hand warm at my waist, until the soup started hissing on the stove.\n\nI rescued the pot. \"This is why we can't have nice things.\"\n\n\"We have a nice thing,\" he said.\n\nIt was so earnest that I laughed, and then kissed him again.\n\n\nWe ate the salty soup anyway.\n\nLater, we carried the dishes to the sink and left them soaking. The shutter would wait until Tamsin could look at it. The laundry would be washed separately next time. The kitchen would probably never be fully organized, and nobody would award us a medal for surviving an ordinary day.\n\nI found a small blue thread still clinging to Maui's sleeve.\n\nI pulled it free.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Nothing. Just proof you actually did the laundry.\"\n\n\"I did.\"\n\n\"Badly.\"\n\n\"Heroically badly.\"\n\nI laughed and leaned against him.\n\nThe moon was bright over Tideglass. The lower landing glimmered with wet stones, and the upper path lanterns burned quietly. Tomorrow there would be meetings and repairs and people who needed help.\n\nTonight there was a pink shirt, a crooked shutter, and the warm breath of someone who wanted to keep learning how to live with me.\n\nI wrote the day in my green notebook.\n\nNot *we accomplished nothing.*\n\nInstead: *We made mistakes, and the house is still home.*","word_count":1554}}};for(const s of lib.series){const byId=edits[s.series_slug]||{};for(const c of s.chapters){const v=byId[c.id];if(!v||c.chapter_number!==v.number)continue;c.content=v.content;c.word_count=v.word_count;c.title=v.title;}}})();
